  {
    "claim": "CORRECTION (2026-08-19, five-run instrumented investigation): the same-session dylib gotcha's MECHANISM was misdiagnosed - the hot-reload watcher IS armed under `newbound mcp` (initialize_all_commands starts it before the subcommand dispatch; its startup banner and inotify watches confirmed by strace). What actually fails is the reload-register seam: on a new-command upsert the watcher detects the change and dlopens the fresh temp copy, yet the command never appears in RUST_COMMANDS (5/5 reproductions, 15-25s waits), and in one run the debouncer thread died mid-println with no panic message. Related known bug (owner): hot-swapping while code from that same library runs in another thread crashes the whole process in server mode. The FACT stands - a command upserted in a live process is not callable in that process - but for new commands in EXISTING libraries too, not only new crates, and not because the watcher is absent",
    "detail": "Consequences unchanged: author in one process, execute in a fresh one (each nb-call IS a fresh process, so the driver idiom is naturally safe); evaluate_rust cannot succeed in ANY single mcp process until the reload-register seam is fixed. interim-process.md section 2 claims this path verified working 2026-08-09 (flowlang 0.3.31 era) - either it regressed by 0.3.33 or that verification measured something else; worth an owner look at FlowLangLibrary::reload (call_mirror's Result is silently discarded - a diagnosability fix regardless). Also: every process start copies each dylib to /tmp/lib<name>_<ts>.so and cleanup rides Drop, so kill/terminate leaks them - 142 accumulated in one session of fresh-process calls.",
    "tags": "hot-reload,mcp,dylib,gotcha",
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1787141356049
  },
  {
    "claim": "newbound_core commands call same-crate commands DIRECTLY (use crate::...::<fn> import + plain function call), never via Command::lookup - the owner's rule, 2026-08-20: indirect invocation is unnecessary inside one crate. Applied to upsert_command/patch_command_body/set_command_imports (-> dev.dev.compile's compile()) and evaluate_rust (-> upsert_command(), invoke_command()), each direct call wrapped in catch_unwind to preserve the {status, kind:\"compile_error\", msg} error shape. Command indirection remains only where the target is runtime-resolved: dev.code.invoke_command, app.app.exec, peer.service.exec, app.service.init. Direct calls return the inner result UNWRAPPED - no Command \"a\" envelope (evaluate_rust's invoke result lost one nesting level).",
    "tags": "dev.code,newbound_core,style,direct-calls",
    "source": {
      "doc": "newbound branch claude/newbound-core-direct-calls-qo26xm (dev.code direct-call refactor)"
    },
    "confidence": "high",
    "time": 1787227760089
  },
  {
    "claim": "CORRECTION to the 2026-08-20 direct-calls claim: a same-crate direct call must preserve the callee's OBSERVABLE result shape. Command::execute returns the generated wrapper's {\"a\": inner} envelope verbatim (flowlang 0.3.34 command.rs/rustcmd.rs - Rust commands never return Err), so replacing it with a direct inner-fn call strips one envelope level; any caller that received that object verbatim breaks. evaluate_rust hit this: its invoke result flows to callers unchanged, so it now re-wraps the direct invoke_command() result in the {\"a\": ...} envelope - external shape is byte-identical to the pre-refactor Command path (verified over MCP). The earlier claim's final sentence ('lost one nesting level') is superseded: NO caller-visible shape changed in the direct-call refactor. upsert_command/patch_command_body/set_command_imports were never affected - they unwrapped and repackaged the compile result, so their outputs were already identical.",
    "tags": "dev.code,newbound_core,direct-calls,correction",
